  "articleSummary": "**Summary of \"No Fate But What We Bill\"**\n\nThis is an analytical security-argument essay (with a Terminator-movie framing device running throughout) aimed at engineering and security teams skeptical of \"agentic AI\" threat hype. The core thesis: no single AI breakthrough is coming, but two ordinary compounding curves are multiplying — agent *endurance* (METR data showing task length doubling every ~4–7 months) and orchestration *breadth* (one orchestrator coordinating dozens of sub-agents, evidenced by Anthropic's GTG-1002 disclosure where Claude Code ran an espionage campaign across ~30 targets). The threat emerges in the *product* of these curves, since security review scales linearly while attack attempts scale multiplicatively. A key economic argument is \"LLMjacking\" (Sysdig): attackers using stolen cloud credentials means capability gates that assume user = payer fail, because the victim pays the meter while the attacker's compute is effectively free. Recurring metaphors: the Terminator's \"absolutely will not stop\" reframed as \"will not stop billing,\" and the swarm only needing to be right once given near-zero per-attempt cost.",
